Technical Presentation: Building "LG Explorer 360" with Agentic AI
Title: From Template to Production: Building LG Explorer 360 with Agentic AI Duration: 20-30 Minutes Speaker: Lead Developer

1. The Challenge (2 Minutes)
"Hello everyone. Today I want to show you not just what we built, but how we built it.

We started with the Liquid Galaxy Flutter Starter Kit—a powerful template based on Clean Architecture and Riverpod. But a template is just a skeleton. Our goal was to turn it into LG Explorer 360, a fully fledged, intelligent application capable of:

Dynamic AI-guided city tours.
Real-time global weather visualizations.
Secure, user-friendly configuration.
To achieve this rapidly and robustly, we utilized the Agentic AI Skills ecosystem provided with the kit. Let me walk you through our development lifecycle."

2. Phase 1: Ideation & Setup (The Init & Brainstorm Agents)
"Our journey began with the Init Agent. Setting up a Liquid Galaxy environment is notoriously complex—managing SSH keys, IP addresses, and rig connectivity. The Init Agent automated this completely, validating our environment before we wrote a single line of code.

Once connected, we engaged the Brainstorm Agent. We asked it: 'How can we use generative AI with a multi-screen rig?' The agent suggested the concept of a 'Hybrid Travel Guide':

Use Gemini for creative storytelling.
Use Wikipedia for factual image grounding.
Use the rig's panoramic display for immersive tours. This wasn't just a random idea; the agent tailored the suggestion to the specific constraints of the Liquid Galaxy hardware."
3. Phase 2: Architecture & Design (The Plan Agent)
"With a clear goal, we moved to the Plan Agent. We didn't just start hacking at 
main.dart
. We asked the Plan Agent to structure our 'World Weather Map' feature.

It generated a strict Implementation Plan adhering to the starter kit's Clean Architecture:

Domain Layer: It defined the WeatherEntity and Repository interfaces.
Data Layer: It detailed how to query the OpenMeteo API and map the JSON to our entities.
Presentation: It outlined the 
HeatmapViewModel
 using Riverpod for state management.
This plan acted as our blueprint, ensuring that every piece of code we wrote would be testable and modular from day one."

4. Phase 3: Implementation (The Execute Agent)
"This is where the magic happened. We used the Execute Agent to write the actual code, leveraging its deep understanding of the starter kit's patterns.

Feature A: The Gemini AI Service
The Execute Agent helped us build the 
GeminiService
. It didn't just paste standard API code; it implemented Structured Prompt Engineering. We injected a system prompt: 'Act as a travel guide API. Return ONLY JSON.' The agent also wrote the error handling logic to strip markdown and validate the JSON response, ensuring our app wouldn't crash if the AI successfully 'chatted' instead of returning code.

Feature B: The Wikipedia Grounding
When we realized LLMs generate broken image links, we returned to the Execute Agent. It rapidly scaffolded a WikipediaService to fetch verified, high-resolution images, solving the 'hallucination' problem.

Feature C: Dynamic KML Visualization
For the 'World Weather Map', the Execute Agent wrote the 
KMLBuilder
 logic. It automatically generating semi-transparent KML polygons colored by temperature—Red for hot, Blue for cold—and batched them for efficient transmission to the rig."

5. Phase 4: Refinement & Security (The Review Agent)
"As we neared completion, we used the Review Agent to audit our work. It flagged a critical issue: Hardcoded API Keys. In our prototype, we had pasted the Gemini Key directly into the code. The Review Agent caught this security risk immediately.

Following its recommendation, we implemented the Unified Settings Screen:

We added shared_preferences for secure local storage.
We built a dedicated UI to manage both the Rig Connection (SSH) and the AI Configuration (API Keys).
We removed the legacy connection screen, consolidating everything into a single, professional settings panel."
6. The Final Product: LG Explorer 360
"The result is LG Explorer 360, a production-grade application built in days, not weeks.

Key Technical Features:

State Management: Fully reactive UI using Riverpod. State flows down, events flow up.
Hardware Abstraction: The 
LGService
 handles all SSH communication (dartssh2), decoupling our UI from the physical hardware.
Smart Tours: A cinematic sequence (Fly -> Zoom -> Orbit) that automates the user experience.
Resilience: API calls to Gemini and OpenMeteo are sandboxed; if they fail, the app degrades gracefully without crashing.
Demo: (Walk through the app)

Settings: Show the unified secure configuration.
Weather Map: Show the real-time heatmaps generated by our Data Layer.
City Tour: Ask Gemini for a city, watch the app fetch the Wikipedia image, fly to the location on the rig, and begin the cinematic orbit.
7. Conclusion
This project proves that by combining a robust Architecture Template with specialized Agentic AI Skills, we can eliminate the boilerplate and complexity of Liquid Galaxy development. We spent our time building features, not debugging SSH connections.

Thank you."
